 The first thing that is not a test to write the fact store: a real file,
 read off the wire, signed, and stored as a content-addressed
 SourceDefinition on the attached disk. It is the ingest half of "delete
 GitHub" -- the writer the compiler's reader (FactDisk) has been waiting
 for, so that a source in the repository was put there by a tool a person
 runs, not only by a test that hardcodes its own work.

 The input is one header line -- path|quire|chapter -- and then the source
 itself, everything after the first newline, up to a NUL. The wire is UTF-8;
 the store is CCE. Those are not the same bytes, and the difference is the
 whole of the hashing rule: hashing is not an I/O function, so a work is
 addressed by its internal CCE bytes, and a tool that stored the UTF-8 off
 the wire would file the work under an address the compiler -- which reads
 and hashes in CCE -- could never look it up by. So the body is decoded from
 UTF-8 and converted through from-unicode before it is stored, the same
 crossing the compiler makes on its own source.

 The decoding is real now: utf8-bytes-to-text (Foreword chapter CCE) walks
 lead bytes and continuation bytes rather than treating each byte as its own
 code point. That earlier shortcut was accurate only over the single-byte
 range, and it failed silently rather than loudly above it -- a continuation
 byte lands in 128..191, which from-unicode answers for as Latin Extended, so
 a two-byte character became two wrong characters and the work was filed under
 the address of text nobody wrote.

 The signing identity is a fixed tool key here. A deployment signs with the
 box's own identity; that this key is well-known does not weaken the
 demonstration -- the signature is real and the gate checks it against
 whatever manifest the reader trusts.

 The store is opened with disk-load and not disk-init, so a work is APPENDED
 to whatever is already on the disk. disk-init builds an empty store, and
 since this tool stores one work per boot, initialising meant every run
 replaced the log the previous run wrote: a disk given three works held only
 the third. Nothing caught that, because the only harness storing anything
 stored exactly one work into a blank disk, and one is the case where
 replacing and appending agree. disk-load reads the superblock when there is
 one and falls back to disk-init when there is not, so a blank disk still
 works and a populated one accumulates.

Section: Identity

  press : Keypair
  press = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-store-tool"))

Section: Entry

  opening : [Device.Block, Console] Nothing = act
    hdr <- read-line
    when hdr
     is None -> print-line-uni "cdx-store: no header on stdin (want path|quire|chapter)"
     is Just (header) -> store-body header (read-serial-cce "src")
  end

 A code point CCE does not carry -- a carriage return, a tab -- has no CCE
 code and from-unicode answers negative one for it. It is dropped, exactly
 as the compiler's own conversion drops it: CCE has no code point for a
 carriage return, so a CRLF file and an LF file are the same text once they
 are inside.

 The serial reader hands back one wire byte per character, so the body is
 gathered into a byte list and handed to the decoder whole -- a UTF-8 sequence
 cannot be decoded a character at a time, which is the entire point.

  raw-to-bytes : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  raw-to-bytes (raw) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else raw-to-bytes raw (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-code (char-at raw i)))

  to-cce : Text -> Text
  to-cce (raw) =
    utf8-bytes-to-text (raw-to-bytes raw 0 (text-length raw) [])

  store-body : Text, Text -> [Device.Block, Console] Nothing
  store-body (header) (raw) = act
    let body = to-cce raw
    in let parts = text-split header "|"
    in if list-length parts < 3
       then print-line-uni "cdx-store: header must be path|quire|chapter"
       else store-parts parts body
  end

  store-parts : List Text, Text -> [Device.Block, Console] Nothing
  store-parts (parts) (body) = act
    let def = SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = "", sd-path = list-at parts 0, sd-quire = list-at parts 1,
      sd-chapter = list-at parts 2, sd-signer = "", sd-signature = [],
      sd-author = "codex-store", sd-timestamp = 1, sd-content = body
    }
    in let signed = sign-definition press def 1
    in let s0 = disk-load
    in let s1 = repo-persist-save-definition s0 signed 1
    in print-line-uni ("stored " & signed.sd-hash & " " & signed.sd-path)
  end

